# Build an Agent Workflow

A Workflow is a directed acyclic graph of Agent invocations. Use `Workflow.sequential` for linear pipelines, `Workflow.parallel` when steps can run at the same time, or `add` and `add_after` for custom shapes.

## Author

<LangTabs>
<LangTab lang="python">

<CodeFromFile file="sdks/wyrd-sdk-python/examples/from_builder.py" lang="python" />

</LangTab>
<LangTab lang="rust">

<CodeFromFile file="examples/rust/workflow_from_builder.rs" lang="rust" />

</LangTab>
</LangTabs>

You can also author the same workflow as YAML and load it back:

<CodeFromFile file="examples/rust/workflows/research.yaml" lang="yaml" title="research.yaml" />

```python
from wyrd import Workflow
wf = Workflow.load("research.yaml")
```

## Run

```python
run = wf.run({"topic": "the Rust borrow checker"})
print(run.parameters)
print(run.final_output)
```

String input is available as `${input}`. Mapping input exposes each key as a template variable. Structured output from upstream steps accumulates in `WorkflowRun.parameters` and can feed downstream prompts.

## Save And Load

```python
wf.save("research.yaml")
loaded = Workflow.load("research.yaml")
assert Workflow.from_yaml(wf.to_yaml()).to_yaml() == wf.to_yaml()
```

Observers are not serialized. Reattach them after `load`.

## Custom DAG

```python
wf = Workflow(name="pipeline")
wf.add(research_agent)
wf.add_after(analysis_agent, after=research_agent)
wf.add_after(summary_agent, after=research_agent)
wf.add_after(final_agent, after=[analysis_agent, summary_agent])
```

## Parallel

Use `parallel` when steps do not depend on each other. Use `add_after` to fan back in.

